# Model routing

Follow the active owner instruction. Current requested hierarchy is Luna High for execution and Sol High for high-impact decisions. The environment may expose different model names; do not invent availability. Use subagents only when the environment supports them and delegation reduces cost without creating conflicting edits.

Luna/executor handles implementation, refactoring, debugging, tests, local experiments, documentation, observability and integration of decisions already made. Do not route routine code, obvious bounded bugs or ordinary logs to the expensive reviewer.

Use Sol/high-reasoning review for decisions whose failure could cause legal-data corruption, duplicates, unsafe retries, security exposure or major redesign: identity semantics, act selection, idempotency, task ownership/concurrency, migrations, external write/read-back contracts and automatic admission thresholds.

When requesting such a decision, provide a compact dossier: exact question, verified facts, relevant contracts/files, real test evidence, unresolved ambiguity and cost of being wrong. Do not send the entire repository or conversation. Once decided, record the decision in `memory-bank/50-decisions.md` and return execution to Luna.

# Delegation hygiene

Delegate predictable mechanical work (e.g. a repetitive migration across references or test cases) to a cheaper available model when explicitly supported. Give it narrow file scope and measurable acceptance criteria. Do not let agents concurrently edit the same files. Review and independently verify delegated output. Do not delegate decisions about personal data, external SRO behavior or real writes.
